/*
===========================================================================
  cl_entview.qc -- point at a trigger or an entity and be told what it is.

  Asked for as: "Make a visual trigger and entity viewer for debug. see which
  functions each trigger touches and what it does, this is for when I need to
  point at a trigger or entity and just see the name of it, and/or the brush
  model, so I can report correctly, make a click to paste in console or
  something."

  The problem it solves is a reporting problem, not a rendering one.  A Source
  map's triggers are invisible by construction -- 452 of surf_tensor2's 622
  brush entities are trigger_* or zone_*, and every one of them is stripped of
  its model server-side so it cannot be seen or hit.  When something misbehaves
  there is no way to say WHICH thing: "the teleport near spawn" is not a bug
  report, and this map has 423 trigger_teleport.

  WHAT IT KNOWS, AND FROM WHERE.  Two sources, and they answer different
  questions:

    the entity LUMP      what the mapper authored -- classname, targetname,
                         model "*N", and every keyvalue.  Read here, on the
                         client, with getentitytoken.
    the live SERVER      what actually happened to it -- which spawn function
                         claimed it, what .solid it ended up with, whether it
                         has a touch, and its entity-I/O output chain.  Fetched
                         with `cmd ent_info`; see SV_EntInfo in sv_zones.qc.

  Both are printed, and where they disagree that IS the finding: a trigger the
  server turned into SOLID_NOT because its class is unimplemented looks exactly
  like a working one from the lump alone.

  BUILT LAZILY, on first use, and thrown away on a map change.  A player who
  never opens the inspector pays one string compare a frame for the latch and
  nothing else -- which matters, because building it spawns an edict per entity
  and this map has 1971 of them.

  WHY IT SPAWNS ITS OWN EDICTS RATHER THAN REUSING cl_sprites.qc's OCCLUDERS.
  Those exist for a different question and are a SUBSET chosen by a cvar:
  vbsp_glare_brush picks which brush entities block a glare, it excludes every
  trigger by design, and at 0 there are none at all.  An inspector that could
  only see what the glare code happens to want is not an inspector.  So this
  builds its own, and the two sets are kept apart by their solidity:

    cl_sprites' occluders     SOLID_BSP        -- seen by MOVE_NOMONSTERS
    these                     SOLID_BSPTRIGGER -- invisible to MOVE_NOMONSTERS
                              + FL_FINDABLE_NONSOLID

  world.c:1924 and :2106 skip anything that is not SOLID_BSP for a
  MOVE_NOMONSTERS trace, and every existing client traceline -- the glare, the
  spotlight, the decal probe, hud_eyetrace -- is MOVE_NOMONSTERS.  So none of
  them can see these, and build 53's behaviour is untouched.  That is the whole
  safety argument and it is why the solidity here is not a free choice.

  The probe trace is MOVE_EVERYTHING, which takes World_ClipToEverything
  (world.c:1905) and hits a SOLID_BSPTRIGGER only when FL_FINDABLE_NONSOLID is
  set (:1918) -- hence the flag.

  IT CAN STILL HIT AN OCCLUDER, because for a solid brush entity the two copies
  are coincident and the trace returns whichever it reached first.  That is not
  a problem to design around: both copies carry the same `model "*N"`, and a
  submodel belongs to exactly one entity, so an unrecognised hit is resolved by
  looking the model string up in the table.  Ev_RowForModel.

  NO QC ARRAYS FOR THE KEYVALUES.  fteqcc costs roughly eight times the data
  size for an array, measured on this project, and 1971 entities times fifteen
  keys is not a thing to pay that on.  Keys and values go into two flat strbufs
  -- heap, allocated at runtime, free -- and a third strbuf holds one
  "<first> <count> <isbrush>" row per entity to index them.  Nothing is
  concatenated, so the temp-string pool is never loaded either.
===========================================================================
*/

#define EV_MAX        4096      // entities kept from the lump
#define EV_PROBE      8192      // how far the crosshair looks

/*
  SOLID_BSPTRIGGER is 22 (pr_common.h:726) and is NOT in defs/cl_defs.qc, which
  is machine-generated by pr_dumpplatform -- a hand-added line there is a line
  that quietly disappears the next time it is regenerated.  Declared locally for
  the same reason cl_netmon.qc declares its #0: builtin locally.

  It is the BSP form deliberately: a plain SOLID_TRIGGER would test the entity's
  axis-aligned bounding box, and a trigger brush built at an angle -- most of a
  surf map's -- would then answer to the crosshair across a box far larger than
  itself.  sv_entities.qc:2916-2925 records the same trap from the other side.
*/
#define EV_SOLID_BSPTRIGGER 22

/*
  Copy to the clipboard.  CSQC may WRITE the clipboard but not read it:
  pr_csqc.c:7173 has clipboard_get commented out with "don't let csqc read the
  clipboard right now. its too risky", while :7174 exports clipboard_set, which
  is PF_cl_clipboard_set at pr_clcmd.c:1052 and is shared verbatim with MenuQC.
  Writing is all this needs.

  Builtin number 0 means "resolve by NAME at load", which is exactly how
  cl_netmon.qc declares getdeltacount and for the same stated reason: this is
  not in the generated defs and putting it there would not survive.

  The first argument is a clipboardtype_t (sys.h:81-85): 0 is CBT_CLIPBOARD, the
  ctrl+V target, and 1 is the X11-style selection buffer.
*/
void(float cliptype, string text) clipboard_set = #0:clipboard_set;
#define EV_CLIPBOARD 0

// ------------------------------------------------------------------- state
strbuf ev_k;                    // every key, flat, in lump order
strbuf ev_v;                    // every value, aligned with ev_k
strbuf ev_ix;                   // per entity: "<first> <count> <isbrush>"
/*
  Whether those three handles are live, tracked explicitly rather than by
  testing the handles for truth.  buf_create hands out indices from ZERO, so a
  perfectly valid buffer can have the handle 0 and `if (ev_k)` would call
  buf_del on it never -- or, worse, on the uninitialised 0 of a fresh session,
  which is a buffer somebody else owns.
*/
float  ev_built;
float  ev_n;                    // entities in the table
string ev_map;                  // the map it was built for; "" = not built

float  ev_brush_n;              // entities with a "*N" model
float  ev_point_n;              // ...and without
float  ev_over;                 // entities past EV_MAX

strbuf ev_cn;                   // classname per entity, for O(1) access
strbuf ev_mdl;                  // ...and its "*N", or "" for a point entity

.float  ev_mine;                // 1 on the edicts this file spawned
.float  ev_row;                 // ...and which table row each one is
/*
  An explicit list, rather than findradius or a findfloat walk, and both of
  those were tried on paper first.

  findradius measures to the entity's ORIGIN PLUS ITS BBOX CENTRE, and it tests
  that point against the radius with no allowance for the entity's own extent --
  so a big trigger_teleport you are standing inside, whose centre is 2000 units
  away, is not found.  Missing the trigger you are standing in is the one case
  this feature exists for.

  findfloat scans the whole edict table per call, so walking 1971 entities that
  way is 1971 scans a frame.

  A linked list built once at load is O(n) with no scanning and lets the cull be
  written properly -- centre distance minus the entity's own radius.
*/
.entity ev_next;
entity  ev_head;

float  ui_ev_open;              // the overlay is up
float  ev_sel;                  // selected row, -1 for none

/*
  BUILD 56 -- the cursor half.  See the essay above Ev_Copy.
*/
float  ui_ev_cursor;            // the inspector holds the mouse (UIC_ENTVIEW)
vector ev_mouse;                // last absolute cursor position, virtual pixels
float  ev_mouse_seen;           // ...and whether one has arrived since it was taken
float  ev_click_pending;        // a world click for Ev_Draw to resolve
float  ev_click_centre;         // ...at screen centre, sized by Ev_Draw (ent_click)
float  ev_hover;                // row under the mouse this frame, -1 for none
vector ev_list_scroll;          // the key list's sui scroll offset
string ev_copied;               // what the last copy was (strzone'd), for the toast
float  ev_copied_at;
vector ev_panel_p;              // where Ev_Panel last drew, so that a click on
vector ev_panel_s;              // the panel never picks the world behind it

// The leaderboard's palette (cl_scores.qc SBC_*), so the two read as one UI.
#define EVC_PANEL '0.07 0.08 0.11'
#define EVC_LINE  '0.30 0.55 0.85'
#define EVC_TEXT  '0.88 0.91 0.96'
#define EVC_DIM   '0.50 0.54 0.60'
#define EVC_HOT   '0.16 0.22 0.32'
#define EVC_SEL   '0.45 0.90 0.50'

/*
  Labels queued by the world pass and drawn by the 2D one; see Ev_Labels.  Small
  and fixed, because a screen with more than this many readable labels on it is
  already unreadable -- the budget is a legibility limit, not a memory one.
*/
#define EV_LABELS 64
vector ev_lab_p[EV_LABELS];
vector ev_lab_c[EV_LABELS];
float  ev_lab_a[EV_LABELS];
string ev_lab_t[EV_LABELS];
float  ev_lab_n;

/*
===========================================================================
  The table
===========================================================================
*/

/*
  Drop the previous map's copies.  The NEXT one is found before the current one
  is removed, because findfloat walks the edict table from `start` and handing
  it an edict that has just been freed is how you get a walk that stops early --
  the same ordering Sprite_FreeOccluders documents.
*/
void() Ev_Free =
{
	local entity e, nxt;

	e = findfloat(world, ev_mine, TRUE);
	while (e)
	{
		nxt = findfloat(e, ev_mine, TRUE);
		remove(e);
		e = nxt;
	}

	ev_head = world;

	if (ev_built)
	{
		buf_del(ev_k);
		buf_del(ev_v);
		buf_del(ev_ix);
		buf_del(ev_cn);
		buf_del(ev_mdl);
		ev_built = FALSE;
	}

	ev_n = ev_brush_n = ev_point_n = ev_over = 0;
	ev_sel = -1;
	ev_hover = -1;
	ev_map = "";
};

/*
  One entity's probe copy.

  SOLID BEFORE SETMODEL, because setmodel links the entity and World_LinkEdict
  reads .solid to decide which of the areagrid's lists it belongs in -- the same
  constraint Sprite_AddOccluder and sv_entities.qc:2546 both record.

  A point entity gets a small box instead.  Source's own editor does the same
  thing and for the same reason: an entity with no geometry still has to be
  selectable, and eight units is the size Hammer draws.  drawmask 0 throughout,
  because nothing here is ever drawn as an entity -- the overlay draws polygons.
*/
void(float row, string mdl, vector org) Ev_AddCopy =
{
	local entity e;
	local vector lo, hi;

	e = spawn();
	e.ev_mine  = TRUE;
	e.ev_row   = row;
	e.movetype = MOVETYPE_NONE;
	e.solid    = EV_SOLID_BSPTRIGGER;
	/*
	  Without this the probe cannot see it at all: World_ClipToEverything
	  rejects a SOLID_TRIGGER or SOLID_BSPTRIGGER that is not flagged findable
	  (world.c:1918), and so does the ordinary areagrid path (:2089-2096).
	*/
	e.flags    = e.flags | FL_FINDABLE_NONSOLID;

	if (substring(mdl, 0, 1) == "*")
	{
		setmodel(e, mdl);
		setorigin(e, org);

		/*
		  BUILD 54, ent_view_solid -- AN A/B THAT COSTS TEN LINES INSTEAD OF A
		  SECOND TEST SLOT.

		  The first in-game run had the probe report "no entity" while aimed at
		  func_brush *19, and reading world.c does not settle which of the two
		  plausible causes it is:

		    0  SOLID_BSPTRIGGER, the designed answer.  Exact brush shape, and
		       World_ClipMoveToEntity (:1259) fetches the real hull for it --
		       but only `&& mdlidx`, and whether a CSQC inline "*N" resolves
		       through csqc_world's Get_CModel is not something static reading
		       can prove.
		    1  SOLID_BBOX with the model's own extents.  The engine cannot
		       decline it: World_ClipToEverything skips SOLID_NOT and skips
		       TRIGGER/BSPTRIGGER without FL_FINDABLE_NONSOLID, and SOLID_BBOX
		       passes both tests unconditionally.  It costs brush-shape
		       precision, which the overlay already approximates with an AABB.

		  BOTH ARE SAFE FOR THE GLARES, which is the property build 53 must not
		  lose: every glare, spotlight and eyetrace uses MOVE_NOMONSTERS, and
		  World_ClipToEverything's `clip->type & MOVE_NOMONSTERS && solid !=
		  SOLID_BSP` (:1930) discards both of these before any hull is touched.

		  MEASURED, AND 1 IS NOW THE DEFAULT.  One run, one vantage, both arms:

		    ent_view_solid 0   ent_here: no entity under the crosshair
		                       table 1997 rows, 1997 copies alive, 1997 with a
		                       box; trace frac 0.0239 hit world
		    ent_view_solid 1   [enthere] class=trigger_teleport model=*36
		                       target="intro_tele_d" ... and the server's
		                       onstarttouch -> intro_fade . Fade () chain

		  Same ray, same frame, 1997 copies alive in both -- so it is not the
		  table and not the linking: a CSQC SOLID_BSPTRIGGER copy of an inline
		  "*N" model is simply not returned by the probe, and the box is.  The
		  cost is brush-shape precision on a rotated brush, which the overlay
		  already approximates with an AABB and says so.
		*/
		if (cvar("ent_view_solid") != 0)
		{
			lo = e.mins;
			hi = e.maxs;
			e.solid = SOLID_BBOX;
			setsize(e, lo, hi);
		}
	}
	else
	{
		e.solid = SOLID_TRIGGER;        // no brush to test; a box is honest
		setorigin(e, org);
		setsize(e, '-8 -8 -8', '8 8 8');
	}

	e.drawmask = 0;

	e.ev_next = ev_head;
	ev_head   = e;
};

/*
  Walk the lump once and build the whole table.

  Deliberately NOT called from CSQC_WorldLoaded.  Every other reader in this
  client walks the lump at load because it has to draw something; this one is a
  debug tool that most sessions never open, and building it costs an edict per
  entity.  So it is built on first use and latched on the map name, which is
  also what makes `ent_reload` a one-line command.
*/
float() Ev_Build =
{
	local string tok, key, val, cn, mdl, map;
	local vector org;
	local float first, count, isbrush, opened;

	map = serverkey("map");
	if (map == "")
		return FALSE;

	Ev_Free();

	ev_k   = buf_create();
	ev_v   = buf_create();
	ev_ix  = buf_create();
	ev_cn  = buf_create();
	ev_mdl = buf_create();
	ev_built = TRUE;
	opened = FALSE;

	getentitytoken(0);
	while ((tok = getentitytoken()) != "")
	{
		if (tok != "{")
			continue;
		opened = TRUE;

		cn = mdl = "";
		org = '0 0 0';
		first = buf_getsize(ev_k);
		count = 0;

		while ((key = getentitytoken()) != "" && key != "}")
		{
			val = getentitytoken();

			if (ev_n < EV_MAX)
			{
				bufstr_add(ev_k, key, TRUE);
				bufstr_add(ev_v, val, TRUE);
				count = count + 1;
			}

			// Lowercase in every VBSP lump observed, so no dual spelling here --
			// unlike Solidity/StartDisabled, which mappers type by hand.
			if      (key == "classname") cn  = val;
			else if (key == "model")     mdl = val;
			else if (key == "origin")    org = Sprite_ParseVec(val);
		}

		if (ev_n >= EV_MAX)
		{
			ev_over = ev_over + 1;
			continue;
		}

		isbrush = (substring(mdl, 0, 1) == "*");
		if (isbrush)
			ev_brush_n = ev_brush_n + 1;
		else
			ev_point_n = ev_point_n + 1;

		bufstr_add(ev_ix, sprintf("%g %g %g", first, count, isbrush), TRUE);
		// Classname and model again, denormalised, because the overlay asks for
		// both of every entity every frame and Ev_Key is a tokenize plus a scan.
		bufstr_add(ev_cn,  cn, TRUE);
		bufstr_add(ev_mdl, isbrush ? mdl : "", TRUE);
		Ev_AddCopy(ev_n, mdl, org);
		ev_n = ev_n + 1;
	}

	/*
	  The same guard the decals and the sprites use: serverkey("map") can lead
	  the entity data by a frame, and latching on a lump that was not readable
	  means never looking again.  cl_portal.qc:522-530 records the sharper form
	  of this -- a WorldLoaded that still held the PREVIOUS map's lump.
	*/
	if (!opened)
	{
		Ev_Free();
		return FALSE;
	}

	ev_map = map;
	return TRUE;
};

// Build if the map changed or nothing has been built yet.  One string compare
// when it is already current.
float() Ev_Ready =
{
	if (ev_map != "" && ev_map == serverkey("map"))
		return TRUE;
	return Ev_Build();
};

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ lookup

// The index row is "<first> <count> <isbrush>", kept as a string rather than
// three float arrays; see the header.
float(float row) Ev_IsBrush =
{
	tokenize(bufstr_get(ev_ix, row));
	return stof(argv(2));
};

// One key's value, or "" if the entity does not carry it.
string(float row, string key) Ev_Key =
{
	local float i, first, n;

	if (row < 0 || row >= ev_n)
		return "";

	tokenize(bufstr_get(ev_ix, row));
	first = stof(argv(0));
	n     = stof(argv(1));

	for (i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1)
		if (bufstr_get(ev_k, first + i) == key)
			return bufstr_get(ev_v, first + i);
	return "";
};

/*
  Which row owns this submodel.

  The fallback for a probe that landed on cl_sprites.qc's occluder copy instead
  of ours -- see the header.  A "*N" names exactly one entity in the lump, so
  this is exact rather than a guess, and it is a linear scan because it runs on
  a keypress and never per frame.
*/
float(string mdl) Ev_RowForModel =
{
	local float i;

	if (mdl == "" || substring(mdl, 0, 1) != "*")
		return -1;
	for (i = 0; i < ev_n; i = i + 1)
		if (Ev_Key(i, "model") == mdl)
			return i;
	return -1;
};

/*
===========================================================================
  The probe
===========================================================================
*/

/*
  What is under the crosshair.  Returns a table row, or -1 for the world or for
  nothing at all.  trace_endpos and trace_ent are left set for the caller.

  MOVE_EVERYTHING rather than MOVE_TRIGGERS, because the answer wanted is "the
  first thing along this line", world included -- reporting a trigger that is
  behind a wall you are looking at would be worse than reporting nothing.
*/
/*
  BUILD 56: the direction is a parameter, because the cursor points along a ray
  through the MOUSE rather than along the view's forward.  Ev_Probe keeps its
  exact build-54 behaviour by passing v_forward.
*/
float(vector dir) Ev_ProbeDir =
{
	local vector eye;
	local float  row;

	if (!Ev_Ready())
		return -1;

	eye = getproperty(VF_ORIGIN);
	traceline(eye, eye + dir * EV_PROBE, MOVE_EVERYTHING, world);

	if (trace_fraction >= 1 || trace_ent == world)
		return -1;

	if (trace_ent.ev_mine)
		return trace_ent.ev_row;

	// Not ours: almost certainly cl_sprites.qc's coincident occluder copy.
	row = Ev_RowForModel(trace_ent.model);
	return row;
};

float() Ev_Probe =
{
	makevectors(getproperty(VF_ANGLES));
	return Ev_ProbeDir(v_forward);
};

/*
  The one-line summary, in the same shape the engine's shader_here uses
  (view.c:2458) -- one greppable line of key=value pairs, so a console dump
  survives being sorted, counted, or pasted into a report, which is the whole
  point of the feature.
*/
/*
  BUILD 56: split in two.  The panel shows the line WITHOUT `at=`, because by the
  time a panel is drawn trace_endpos belongs to whichever trace ran last, and a
  stale position in a bug report is worse than none.  ent_here's line is
  byte-identical to build 54's, so anything already grepping for it still works.
*/
string(float row) Ev_Summary =
{
	local string cn, tn, mdl, tgt;

	if (row < 0 || row >= ev_n)
		return "[enthere] nothing";

	cn  = Ev_Key(row, "classname");
	tn  = Ev_Key(row, "targetname");
	mdl = Ev_Key(row, "model");
	tgt = Ev_Key(row, "target");

	return sprintf("[enthere] class=%s model=%s targetname=\"%s\" target=\"%s\"",
	               (cn == "") ? "?" : cn,
	               (mdl == "") ? "-" : mdl,
	               tn, tgt);
};

string(float row) Ev_Line =
{
	if (row < 0 || row >= ev_n)
		return "[enthere] nothing";
	return sprintf("%s at=%.0f %.0f %.0f", Ev_Summary(row),
	               trace_endpos_x, trace_endpos_y, trace_endpos_z);
};

/*
  Print an entity's whole authored keyvalue block, one per line and indented, so
  a report can carry the thing verbatim rather than a paraphrase of it.
*/
void(float row) Ev_PrintKeys =
{
	local float i, first, n;

	if (row < 0 || row >= ev_n)
		return;

	tokenize(bufstr_get(ev_ix, row));
	first = stof(argv(0));
	n     = stof(argv(1));

	for (i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1)
		print(sprintf("    %-24s %s\n", bufstr_get(ev_k, first + i),
		                                bufstr_get(ev_v, first + i)));
};

/*
  Select a row and say everything about it: the greppable line, every key, the
  clipboard, and the server's half.  Shared by ent_here (the crosshair) and by a
  click with the cursor taken (the mouse), so the two cannot drift apart.

  IT MUST RUN IMMEDIATELY AFTER THE PROBE'S TRACE -- Ev_Line reads trace_endpos
  and the server request reads trace_ent's box -- and both callers do: nothing
  between the traceline and this call traces again.
*/
void(float row) Ev_Report =
{
	local string line;
	local vector mid;

	ev_sel = row;
	ev_list_scroll = '0 0 0';

	line = Ev_Line(row);
	print(sprintf("%s\n", line));
	Ev_PrintKeys(row);
	clipboard_set(EV_CLIPBOARD, line);
	print("^2ent_here^7: copied to the clipboard\n");

	/*
	  And ask the SERVER what it made of it.  The lump says what the mapper
	  authored; only the server knows which spawn function claimed the entity,
	  what solidity it ended up with, and what its entity-I/O chain does.  See
	  SV_EntInfo.  Forwarded with `cmd` for the reason cl_zones.qc:181-188
	  gives: an unknown console command never reaches SV_ParseClientCommand.

	  THE BBOX CENTRE, not trace_endpos.  The impact point lies exactly ON the
	  entity's surface, so the server's containment test is decided by floating
	  point at the boundary and can fall either way.  The centre of the box the
	  client just hit is always inside the box the server will compare against,
	  because both are the same submodel's absmin/absmax.
	*/
	mid = (trace_ent.absmin + trace_ent.absmax) * 0.5;
	localcmd(sprintf("cmd ent_info %s %.1f %.1f %.1f\n",
	                 bufstr_get(ev_cn, row), mid_x, mid_y, mid_z));
};

/*
  ent_here: the probe, printed and copied.

  The clipboard copy is the half that was actually asked for -- "make a click to
  paste in console or something".  There is no builtin that can push text into
  the console's input line (Key_ConsolePaste is engine-private, keys.c:2249), so
  the clipboard is the mechanism: one ctrl+V puts it wherever the report is
  being written.
*/
void() Ev_Here =
{
	local float  row, live, sized;
	local entity p;

	if (!Ev_Ready())
	{
		print("^1ent_here^7: no map entity data yet\n");
		return;
	}

	row = Ev_Probe();
	if (row < 0)
	{
		/*
		  BUILD 54.  SAY WHY, not just that.

		  The first in-game run of this printed "no entity under the crosshair"
		  three times, one of them aimed dead centre at func_brush *19 from 200
		  units, and there was no way to tell from that line whether the table
		  was empty, the probe copies were never linked, or the trace stopped on
		  the world in front of them.  Those are three different bugs wearing
		  one message, and the message is the only output this tool has.

		  So the failure branch reports the state it depends on: how many rows
		  the table holds, how many probe copies are alive, how many of those
		  got a non-degenerate box, and what the trace actually did.  A tool
		  whose entire purpose is letting someone report a bug accurately ought
		  to be able to report its own.
		*/
		live = sized = 0;
		for (p = ev_head; p != world; p = p.ev_next)
		{
			live = live + 1;
			if (p.absmax != p.absmin)
				sized = sized + 1;
		}

		print(sprintf("^3ent_here^7: no entity under the crosshair (%s)\n",
		              (trace_fraction < 1) ? "the trace stopped on world geometry -- try shader_here" : "nothing within range"));
		print(sprintf("^3ent_here^7: table %g rows (%g brush, %g point), %g probe copies alive, %g with a box\n",
		              ev_n, ev_brush_n, ev_point_n, live, sized));
		print(sprintf("^3ent_here^7: trace frac %.4f endpos %v hit %s\n",
		              trace_fraction, trace_endpos,
		              (trace_ent == world) ? "world" : trace_ent.classname));
		/*
		  BUILD 56.  THE COMMONEST "NO ENTITY" IS NOT A FAILURE, and it was
		  reported as one: "It's an entity, so I can't tell you the texture and
		  the ent_here is broken", aimed at the rock clusters on surf_fantasy.
		  Those are STATIC PROPS.  A Source map keeps them in the sprp game lump,
		  not the entity lump, so this table can never contain one -- and the
		  hl2 plugin builds them into the world's collision, which is why the
		  trace says world.  Naming them is the surface probe's job, not this
		  one's; engine patch 291 (another session's) makes shader_here do it.
		*/
		if (trace_fraction < 1)
			print("^3ent_here^7: a static prop is not an entity (props live in the map's sprp lump, not the entity lump) -- aim at it and use shader_here\n");
		return;
	}

	Ev_Report(row);
};

/*
===========================================================================
  The overlay

  ITS OWN LINE AND FACE DRAWERS, and not cl_zones.qc's, which is a real
  temptation because they are eleven lines apiece and already written.  They
  read zone_cv_shader and zone_cv_thickness, two file-scope globals that
  Zone_Draw refreshes from cvars at the top of every frame it draws -- and it
  returns early when zone_draw is 0.  Calling them from here would read whatever
  those globals last held, which on a map where the zone overlay is off is
  whatever the previous map left.  A stale shader name is a wrong-looking line;
  a stale thickness of zero is an invisible one.  Eleven lines is the cheaper
  half of that trade.
===========================================================================
*/

// Exactly two vertices between Begin and End is what selects BEF_LINES.
void(vector a, vector b, vector col, float alpha) Ev_Line3 =
{
	R_BeginPolygon("");
	R_PolygonVertex(a, '0 0 0', col, alpha);
	R_PolygonVertex(b, '0 0 0', col, alpha);
	R_EndPolygon();
};

/*
  The twelve edges of an axis-aligned box.

  AABB AND NOT THE BRUSH ITSELF, stated plainly because it matters when you are
  reading one: a trigger built at an angle has an axis-aligned bounding box
  larger than the brush, so the outline is an over-estimate for those.  It is
  the same box the SERVER links and the same one Hammer draws for selection, and
  getting the true brush faces would mean walking the submodel's surfaces per
  frame.  When the outline and the behaviour disagree, believe the behaviour.
*/
void(vector mn, vector mx, vector col, float alpha) Ev_Box =
{
	local vector a, b;

	// bottom ring
	a = mn;                        b = [mx_x, mn_y, mn_z]; Ev_Line3(a, b, col, alpha);
	a = [mx_x, mn_y, mn_z];        b = [mx_x, mx_y, mn_z]; Ev_Line3(a, b, col, alpha);
	a = [mx_x, mx_y, mn_z];        b = [mn_x, mx_y, mn_z]; Ev_Line3(a, b, col, alpha);
	a = [mn_x, mx_y, mn_z];        b = mn;                 Ev_Line3(a, b, col, alpha);

	// top ring
	a = [mn_x, mn_y, mx_z];        b = [mx_x, mn_y, mx_z]; Ev_Line3(a, b, col, alpha);
	a = [mx_x, mn_y, mx_z];        b = mx;                 Ev_Line3(a, b, col, alpha);
	a = mx;                        b = [mn_x, mx_y, mx_z]; Ev_Line3(a, b, col, alpha);
	a = [mn_x, mx_y, mx_z];        b = [mn_x, mn_y, mx_z]; Ev_Line3(a, b, col, alpha);

	// the four verticals
	a = mn;                        b = [mn_x, mn_y, mx_z]; Ev_Line3(a, b, col, alpha);
	a = [mx_x, mn_y, mn_z];        b = [mx_x, mn_y, mx_z]; Ev_Line3(a, b, col, alpha);
	a = [mx_x, mx_y, mn_z];        b = mx;                 Ev_Line3(a, b, col, alpha);
	a = [mn_x, mx_y, mn_z];        b = [mn_x, mx_y, mx_z]; Ev_Line3(a, b, col, alpha);
};

/*
  Colour by family, so a screenshot of the overlay is readable without reading
  every label.  The families are the ones sv_entities.qc's catch-all already
  sorts entities into, which is the distinction that actually matters when
  something misbehaves: is this thing solid, is it a trigger, or did the server
  decline it entirely.
*/
vector(string cn) Ev_Colour =
{
	if (substring(cn, 0, 8) == "trigger_") return '1.00 0.35 0.35';  // red
	if (substring(cn, 0, 5) == "zone_")    return '1.00 0.65 0.20';  // amber
	if (substring(cn, 0, 5) == "func_")    return '0.35 0.80 1.00';  // blue
	if (substring(cn, 0, 4) == "env_")     return '0.60 1.00 0.60';  // green
	if (substring(cn, 0, 5) == "info_")    return '0.80 0.80 0.80';  // grey
	if (substring(cn, 0, 5) == "light")    return '1.00 1.00 0.55';  // yellow
	return '0.75 0.55 1.00';                                          // violet
};

void() Ev_Draw =
{
	local entity e;
	local vector eye, fwd, mn, mx, mid, col, sp, vsize, mnear, mfar, mdir;
	local float  maxd, rad, dd, alpha, drawn, budget, labels, row, hover;
	local string cn, mdl;

	if (!ui_ev_open)
		return;
	if (!Ev_Ready())
		return;

	// QuakeC locals are not zeroed; fteqcc warns F302 if you forget.
	drawn = 0;
	vsize = getproperty(VF_SCREENVSIZE);
	eye = getproperty(VF_ORIGIN);
	makevectors(getproperty(VF_ANGLES));
	fwd = v_forward;

	maxd = cvar("ent_view_dist");
	if (maxd <= 0)
		maxd = 1024;
	budget = cvar("ent_view_max");
	if (budget <= 0)
		budget = 192;
	labels = cvar("ent_view_labels");

	/*
	  What is being pointed at, so it can be drawn differently.  One trace.

	  BUILD 56: with the cursor taken that is the MOUSE, and a click queued by
	  Ev_InputEvent is resolved here rather than there, because this runs
	  before renderscene for the frame being drawn -- the only place
	  unproject's matrices are this frame's.  See the cursor essay.
	*/
	if (ui_ev_cursor)
	{
		hover = -1;
		// A scripted centre click, sized here because VF_SCREENVSIZE is only
		// certain to be this frame's inside the view update.
		if (ev_click_centre)
		{
			ev_mouse = [vsize_x * 0.5, vsize_y * 0.5];
			ev_click_centre = FALSE;
		}
		if (ev_mouse_seen)
		{
			mnear = unproject([ev_mouse_x, ev_mouse_y, 0]);
			mfar  = unproject([ev_mouse_x, ev_mouse_y, 0.5]);
			mdir  = mfar - mnear;
			// A NaN compares false, so this also rejects a degenerate unproject.
			if (vlen(mdir) > 0.001)
			{
				hover = Ev_ProbeDir(normalize(mdir));
				if (ev_click_pending)
				{
					if (hover >= 0)
						Ev_Report(hover);
					else
						print(sprintf("^3ent_cursor^7: no entity under the cursor (trace frac %.4f) -- a static prop is not an entity; aim the crosshair at it and use shader_here\n",
						              trace_fraction));
				}
			}
		}
		ev_click_pending = FALSE;
		ev_hover = hover;
	}
	else
		hover = Ev_Probe();

	e = ev_head;
	while (e && drawn < budget)
	{
		row = e.ev_row;
		mn  = e.absmin;
		mx  = e.absmax;
		mid = (mn + mx) * 0.5;
		rad = vlen(mx - mid);

		dd = vlen(mid - eye) - rad;
		if (dd > maxd)
		{
			e = e.ev_next;
			continue;
		}
		// Behind the camera, allowing for the entity's own size -- the same two
		// tests Zone_Draw and Sprite_Draw use, for the same reason: a CSQC
		// polygon gets no PVS and no frustum cull from the engine.
		if (fwd * (mid - eye) < -rad)
		{
			e = e.ev_next;
			continue;
		}

		cn  = bufstr_get(ev_cn,  row);
		col = Ev_Colour(cn);

		if (row == hover)
		{
			col   = '1 1 1';
			alpha = 1;
		}
		else if (row == ev_sel && ui_ev_cursor)
		{
			// The entity the panel is describing, so the two can be matched up.
			col   = EVC_SEL;
			alpha = 1;
		}
		else
			alpha = 0.55;

		Ev_Box(mn, mx, col, alpha);
		drawn = drawn + 1;

		/*
		  The label, projected.  project() divides by w with NO zero guard
		  (pr_csqc.c:2075-2077), so a point on the eye plane returns a NaN into
		  QC and a NaN reaches drawstring -- cl_watch.qc:2551-2555 records this
		  and puts its own near plane at 8 rather than the engine's 4.  The
		  dot-product test here is that guard and it runs BEFORE the projection,
		  not after it.
		*/
		if (labels && ev_lab_n < EV_LABELS && (fwd * (mid - eye)) > 8)
		{
			sp = project(mid);
			// getproperty(VF_SCREENVSIZE), not cvar("vid_conwidth"): fteqcc
			// warns F333 that the cvar form returns a compatibility value that
			// need not match the real one.
			if (sp_x > -128 && sp_y > -32 &&
			    sp_x < vsize_x + 128 && sp_y < vsize_y + 32)
			{
				mdl = bufstr_get(ev_mdl, row);
				ev_lab_p[ev_lab_n] = sp;
				ev_lab_c[ev_lab_n] = col;
				ev_lab_a[ev_lab_n] = alpha;
				ev_lab_t[ev_lab_n] = (mdl == "") ? cn : strcat(cn, " ", mdl);
				ev_lab_n = ev_lab_n + 1;
			}
		}

		e = e.ev_next;
	}
};

/*
  The labels, and they are a SECOND pass for a reason that is structural rather
  than tidy: drawstring is 2D and the boxes are world polygons.  Everything
  above runs before renderscene, because R_BeginPolygon queues into the scene
  and that is what lets a volume behind a wall be hidden by it.  Text queued
  there would be drawn UNDER the scene and never seen.

  So the walk happens once, the boxes go out immediately, and the label
  positions -- already projected while the view matrix was set up -- are held
  until after renderscene.  Two passes over the list would cost the cull twice;
  a handful of small arrays costs nothing and keeps the cull in one place.
*/
void() Ev_Labels =
{
	local float i;

	for (i = 0; i < ev_lab_n; i = i + 1)
		HUD_Text(ev_lab_p[i], ev_lab_t[i], 8, ev_lab_c[i], ev_lab_a[i]);
	ev_lab_n = 0;
};

/*
===========================================================================
  BUILD 56 -- THE CURSOR HALF

  Asked for as "make a click to paste in console or something", and chosen as
  the full inspector with the mouse.  Build 54 shipped the keyboard half:
  ent_here probes the crosshair, prints and copies.  This is the half where you
  point with the MOUSE, click the thing, and click any line of what it says to
  copy that one line.

  ONE MORE OWNER OF ONE CURSOR, AND THE REGISTER IS WHAT MAKES THAT CHEAP.  The
  claim/release bitmask in cl_hud.qc exists so that a fifth panel does not have
  to edit the four before it, and UIC_ENTVIEW = 16 was reserved there for this
  file.  Claim on the way in, release on the way out, and the game gets the
  mouse back exactly when the last claim drops.

  BUT A CLAIM IS NOT ENOUGH TO SHARE THE SCREEN, because sui has ONE hit-test
  list and sui_begin empties it (sui_sys.qc:1037-1042).  Two panels bracketing
  sui in one frame means the second owns every click, whatever is drawn in
  front.  So, exactly like the layout editor (cl_hudedit.qc HUD_EditOpen):
  refuse to take the cursor while the replay chrome, the leaderboard, the
  editor, or a results card that has taken the cursor is up -- and let go of it
  the frame one of them appears.  A results card that is merely ON SCREEN is
  not a blocker: it draws with raw drawfill and only opens a sui frame once it
  has been asked for the mouse (the essay above Results_Draw).

  THE PICK IS RESOLVED IN Ev_Draw, NOT IN THE INPUT HANDLER.  unproject reads the
  current view's inverse projection (pr_csqc.c PF_cs_unproject) and an input
  event arrives between frames, so a click only raises a flag and the ray is
  built on the next frame, before renderscene, where the matrices are that
  frame's.  unproject's depth runs 0..1 with the far plane at infinity (it clamps
  1 to 0.999999), so the direction comes from two points at depth 0 and 0.5
  rather than from a "far" point that is numerically nowhere.

  A CLICK ON THE PANEL NEVER PICKS THROUGH IT.  The panel's background is
  registered as a hit element before any row, so sui reports a hit for empty
  panel space too; and the world pick additionally requires the release point to
  lie outside the panel's rectangle, so a press on a row dragged off and
  released over the panel is still not a click on whatever is behind it.

  `ent_click <x> <y>`, `ent_copyrow <n>` and `ent_copyall` drive those same code
  paths from a script, because a cfg cannot move the mouse and a feature that can
  only be exercised by hand is one nobody re-verifies.
===========================================================================
*/

/*
  Put a string on the clipboard and remember a short description of it for the
  panel's toast.  `what` is zoned because the toast outlives the frame the click
  happened in, and a tempstring does not.
*/
void(string s, string what) Ev_Copy =
{
	if (s == "")
		return;
	clipboard_set(EV_CLIPBOARD, s);
	if (ev_copied != "")
		strunzone(ev_copied);
	ev_copied = strzone(what);
	ev_copied_at = cltime;
	print(sprintf("^2ent_cursor^7: copied %s\n", what));
};

/*
  "key" "value" -- the entity lump's own syntax, so a pasted line is valid entity
  text and survives being dropped straight into a report or an .ent file.
*/
void(float idx) Ev_CopyRow =
{
	local float  first, n;
	local string s;

	if (ev_sel < 0 || ev_sel >= ev_n)
	{
		print("^3ent_copyrow^7: nothing selected\n");
		return;
	}
	tokenize(bufstr_get(ev_ix, ev_sel));
	first = stof(argv(0));
	n     = stof(argv(1));
	if (idx < 0 || idx >= n)
	{
		print(sprintf("^3ent_copyrow^7: no row %g -- this entity has %g\n", idx, n));
		return;
	}
	s = sprintf("\"%s\" \"%s\"", bufstr_get(ev_k, first + idx), bufstr_get(ev_v, first + idx));
	Ev_Copy(s, s);
};

// The whole block as entity-lump text, with the summary line above it.
void() Ev_CopyAll =
{
	local float  first, n, i;
	local string s, hdr;

	if (ev_sel < 0 || ev_sel >= ev_n)
	{
		print("^3ent_copyall^7: nothing selected\n");
		return;
	}
	// Ev_Summary tokenizes, so it goes FIRST and the index row is read after it.
	hdr = Ev_Summary(ev_sel);
	tokenize(bufstr_get(ev_ix, ev_sel));
	first = stof(argv(0));
	n     = stof(argv(1));

	s = strcat(hdr, "\n{\n");
	for (i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1)
		s = strcat(s, sprintf("\"%s\" \"%s\"\n", bufstr_get(ev_k, first + i),
		                                         bufstr_get(ev_v, first + i)));
	s = strcat(s, "}\n");
	Ev_Copy(s, sprintf("%s and its %g keys", hdr, n));
};

// Which panel stands in the way, or "" when none does.
string() Ev_CursorBlocker =
{
	if (ui_he_open)   return "the layout editor";
	if (rec_sb_open)  return "the leaderboard";
	if (rec_wt_open)  return "a replay";
	if (ui_rs_cursor) return "the results card";
	return "";
};

void() Ev_CursorOff =
{
	ev_click_pending = FALSE;
	ev_click_centre  = FALSE;
	if (!ui_ev_cursor)
		return;
	ui_ev_cursor  = FALSE;
	ev_mouse_seen = FALSE;
	ev_hover      = -1;
	UI_CursorRelease(UIC_ENTVIEW);
};

void() Ev_CursorOn =
{
	local string why;

	if (ui_ev_cursor)
		return;

	why = Ev_CursorBlocker();
	if (why != "")
	{
		print(sprintf("^3ent_cursor^7: close %s first\n", why));
		return;
	}
	if (!Ev_Ready())
	{
		print("^1ent_cursor^7: no map entity data yet\n");
		return;
	}

	ui_ev_open       = TRUE;
	ui_ev_cursor     = TRUE;
	ev_mouse_seen    = FALSE;
	ev_click_pending = FALSE;
	ev_hover         = -1;
	// No hold or release left behind by whichever panel last used sui.
	sui_reset_actions();
	UI_CursorClaim(UIC_ENTVIEW);

	print("^5ent_cursor^7: click an entity to select it, click a line to copy it; ESC or ent_cursor to finish\n");
};

/*
  The panel.  Drawn after the results card and before the layout editor in
  cl_main.qc, which with the refusals above makes its sui bracket the last one in
  any frame the cursor is taken -- the property the essay says clicks depend on.
*/
void(vector scr) Ev_Panel =
{
	local vector p, tp, lp, rs;
	local float  w, h, pad, tsz, rsz, rowh, keyw, first, n, idx, hot, a, chipw;
	local string why, rid, hdr;

	if (!ui_ev_cursor)
		return;

	why = Ev_CursorBlocker();
	if (why != "")
	{
		Ev_CursorOff();
		print(sprintf("^3ent_cursor^7: let go of the mouse -- %s opened\n", why));
		return;
	}

	Font_Set(ui_font_mono);
	pad  = 8 * ui_cv_scale;
	tsz  = Font_Px(9 * ui_cv_scale);
	rsz  = Font_Px(8 * ui_cv_scale);
	rowh = rsz + 6 * ui_cv_scale;
	w = 460 * ui_cv_scale;
	if (w > scr_x * 0.5)
		w = scr_x * 0.5;
	h = scr_y - 200 * ui_cv_scale;
	p = [scr_x - w - 16 * ui_cv_scale, 96 * ui_cv_scale, 0];
	ev_panel_p = p;
	ev_panel_s = [w, h, 0];

	sui_begin(scr_x, scr_y);

	// The background FIRST, so it is below every row in sui's front-to-back hit
	// order and a click on empty panel space still counts as a hit.
	sui_action_element(p, [w, h], "ev_bg", sui_noop);
	sui_fill(p, [w, h], EVC_PANEL, 0.92, 0);
	sui_border_box(p, [w, h], 1, EVC_LINE, 0.6, 0);

	tp = p + [pad, pad];
	sui_text(tp, [tsz, tsz], "entity inspector", EVC_TEXT, 1, 0);
	tp_y = tp_y + tsz + pad * 0.5;
	sui_text(tp, [rsz, rsz], "click an entity; click a line to copy it; esc to close", EVC_DIM, 1, 0);
	tp_y = tp_y + rsz + pad;

	if (ev_sel < 0 || ev_sel >= ev_n)
	{
		sui_text(tp, [rsz, rsz], "nothing selected", EVC_DIM, 1, 0);
		tp_y = tp_y + rowh;
		if (ev_hover >= 0 && ev_hover < ev_n)
			sui_text(tp, [rsz, rsz], strcat("under the cursor: ", bufstr_get(ev_cn, ev_hover),
			                                " ", bufstr_get(ev_mdl, ev_hover)), EVC_TEXT, 1, 0);
	}
	else
	{
		// The summary line, which copies itself.
		hdr = Ev_Summary(ev_sel);
		sui_action_element(tp, [w - pad * 2, rowh], "ev_hdr", sui_noop);
		if (sui_is_hovered("ev_hdr"))
			sui_fill(tp, [w - pad * 2, rowh], EVC_HOT, 1, 0);
		sui_text(tp + [2, (rowh - rsz) * 0.5], [rsz, rsz], hdr, EVC_SEL, 1, 0);
		if (sui_is_released("ev_hdr"))
			Ev_Copy(hdr, hdr);
		tp_y = tp_y + rowh + pad * 0.5;

		chipw = 110 * ui_cv_scale;
		sui_action_element(tp, [chipw, rowh], "ev_all", sui_noop);
		sui_fill(tp, [chipw, rowh], sui_is_hovered("ev_all") ? EVC_HOT : EVC_PANEL, 1, 0);
		sui_border_box(tp, [chipw, rowh], 1, EVC_LINE, 0.6, 0);
		sui_text(tp + [pad * 0.5, (rowh - rsz) * 0.5], [rsz, rsz], "copy all", EVC_TEXT, 1, 0);
		if (sui_is_released("ev_all"))
			Ev_CopyAll();
		tp_y = tp_y + rowh + pad;

		// Ev_Summary above tokenized; the index row is read after it, and nothing
		// in the loop below uses argv except Ev_CopyRow, which reads its own.
		tokenize(bufstr_get(ev_ix, ev_sel));
		first = stof(argv(0));
		n     = stof(argv(1));
		keyw  = stringwidth("vrad_brush_cast_shadows  ", TRUE, [rsz, rsz]);

		// Room for the toast underneath.
		rs = [w - pad * 2, (p_y + h - pad) - tp_y - rowh * 1.5, 0];
		sui_list_view_begin("ev_keys", tp, rs, [rs_x - 8 * ui_cv_scale, rowh], n,
		                    ev_list_scroll, [0, 8 * ui_cv_scale]);
		lp = '0 0 0';
		for (idx = sui_list_item(lp); idx > -1; idx = sui_list_item(lp))
		{
			rid = strcat("ev_k", ftos(idx));
			sui_action_element(lp, [rs_x - 8 * ui_cv_scale, rowh], rid, sui_noop);
			hot = sui_is_hovered(rid);
			if (hot)
				sui_fill(lp, [rs_x - 8 * ui_cv_scale, rowh], EVC_HOT, 1, 0);
			sui_text(lp + [2, (rowh - rsz) * 0.5], [rsz, rsz],
			         bufstr_get(ev_k, first + idx), hot ? EVC_TEXT : EVC_DIM, 1, 0);
			sui_text(lp + [2 + keyw, (rowh - rsz) * 0.5], [rsz, rsz],
			         bufstr_get(ev_v, first + idx), EVC_TEXT, 1, 0);
			if (sui_is_released(rid))
				Ev_CopyRow(idx);
		}
		sui_list_view_end();
	}

	a = 1 - (cltime - ev_copied_at) / 2;
	if (ev_copied != "" && a > 0)
	{
		if (a > 1)
			a = 1;
		sui_text([p_x + pad, p_y + h - pad - rsz], [rsz, rsz],
		         strcat("copied  ", ev_copied), EVC_SEL, a, 0);
	}

	sui_end();
	Font_Reset();
};

/*
  Input, reached only once the cursor has been taken -- the chain in cl_main.qc
  tests ui_ev_cursor, so an overlay that is merely on screen swallows nothing.

  IE_MOUSEABS RETURNS TRUE UNCONDITIONALLY, for the reason Scores_InputEvent
  gives: the engine zeroes the accumulated mouse delta only when CSQC returns
  TRUE, and a handler that forgets leaves the view turning under the cursor.
  MOUSE1 and MOUSE2 are swallowed because default.cfg binds them to +duck and
  +jump.  Everything else falls through, so you can still walk while pointing.
*/
float(float evtype, float scanx, float chary, float devid) Ev_InputEvent =
{
	local float hit;

	if (!ui_ev_cursor)
		return FALSE;

	if (evtype == IE_MOUSEABS)
	{
		ev_mouse = [scanx, chary];
		ev_mouse_seen = TRUE;
		sui_input_event(evtype, scanx, chary, devid);
		return TRUE;
	}

	if (evtype != IE_KEYDOWN && evtype != IE_KEYUP)
		return FALSE;

	if (scanx == K_MOUSE1)
	{
		hit = sui_input_event(evtype, scanx, chary, devid);
		// A release no panel element took, and not over the panel at all, is a
		// click on the world.  The pick itself waits for Ev_Draw.
		if (evtype == IE_KEYUP && !hit && ev_mouse_seen &&
		    !is_2dpoint_in_bbox(ev_mouse, ev_panel_p, ev_panel_s))
			ev_click_pending = TRUE;
		return TRUE;
	}
	if (scanx == K_MOUSE2)
		return TRUE;

	// The key list scrolls from sui's input buffer (sui_scroll_view_begin reads
	// K_MWHEELUP/DOWN out of it), and only a KEYDOWN is ever put there.
	if (scanx == K_MWHEELUP || scanx == K_MWHEELDOWN)
	{
		if (evtype == IE_KEYDOWN)
			sui_input_event(evtype, scanx, chary, devid);
		return TRUE;
	}

	if (evtype == IE_KEYDOWN && scanx == K_ESCAPE)
	{
		Ev_CursorOff();
		print("^5ent_cursor^7: off\n");
		return TRUE;
	}
	return FALSE;
};

/*
  MOUSE3 asks for the cursor while the overlay is up, matching the results card's
  gesture: MOUSE1/MOUSE2 are duck and jump, and MOUSE3 is the only free click in
  the shipped config.  The card keeps it while a card is up -- it was there
  first, and Results_PassiveKey is offered the press before this one is.
*/
float(float scanx) Ev_PassiveKey =
{
	if (scanx != K_MOUSE3 || !ui_ev_open || ui_ev_cursor || ui_rs_open)
		return FALSE;
	Ev_CursorOn();
	return ui_ev_cursor;
};

/*
===========================================================================
  Console
===========================================================================
*/
void() Ev_Register =
{
	/*
	  ent_here and ent_view, not shader_here/solid_here/light_here -- those three
	  are the engine's own (view.c:3403-3405) and registercommand silently
	  refuses a name that already exists, so shadowing one would fail without
	  saying so.  The three of them are the sibling feature for world SURFACES;
	  this is the one for ENTITIES, which is the gap solid_here names in its own
	  comment at view.c:2610-2613.
	*/
	registercommand("ent_here");
	registercommand("ent_view");
	registercommand("ent_reload");
	// Build 56: the cursor, and the scripted forms of what the mouse does.
	registercommand("ent_cursor");
	registercommand("ent_click");
	registercommand("ent_copyrow");
	registercommand("ent_copyall");

	/*
	  Registered beside the read, which is this project's rule and has silently
	  killed three features when it was broken: cvar() auto-creates the name as
	  "" the first time it is read, and registercvar then DECLINES, so a non-zero
	  default registered somewhere later never lands.

	  Not CVAR_ARCHIVE.  This is a debug overlay, not a look preference: it
	  should not survive into a session where somebody has forgotten they turned
	  it on, and ent_view itself is deliberately not archived either.
	*/
	registercvar("ent_view_dist", "1024");   // how far the volumes are drawn
	registercvar("ent_view_max", "192");     // volumes per frame, a fill budget
	registercvar("ent_view_labels", "1");    // draw the classname at each centre
	// 1 = SOLID_BBOX probe copies of the model's own extents (the default, and
	// the only arm the probe actually returns -- measured, both arms, one run),
	// 0 = SOLID_BSPTRIGGER, exact brush shape, kept because it is the shape the
	// design wanted and the day it starts working it is the better answer.
	// See the essay in Ev_AddCopy; both are invisible to MOVE_NOMONSTERS, which
	// is what keeps them off the glare traces.
	registercvar("ent_view_solid", "1");
};

float(string cmd) Ev_ConsoleCommand =
{
	if (argv(0) == "ent_here")
	{
		Ev_Here();
		return TRUE;
	}

	if (argv(0) == "ent_reload")
	{
		Ev_Free();
		if (Ev_Ready())
			print(sprintf("^2ent_reload^7: %g entities (%g brush, %g point%s)\n",
			              ev_n, ev_brush_n, ev_point_n,
			              ev_over ? sprintf(", %g over EV_MAX", ev_over) : ""));
		else
			print("^1ent_reload^7: no entity data\n");
		return TRUE;
	}

	if (argv(0) == "ent_view")
	{
		if (!Ev_Ready())
		{
			print("^1ent_view^7: no map entity data yet\n");
			return TRUE;
		}
		ui_ev_open = !ui_ev_open;
		// Closing the overlay closes the cursor with it: a panel you cannot see
		// must not keep the mouse.
		if (!ui_ev_open)
			Ev_CursorOff();
		print(sprintf("^5ent_view^7: %s -- %g entities (%g brush, %g point)%s\n",
		              ui_ev_open ? "on" : "off", ev_n, ev_brush_n, ev_point_n,
		              ui_ev_open ? "; mouse3 or ent_cursor for the cursor" : ""));
		if (ev_over)
			print(sprintf("^3ent_view^7: %g entities past EV_MAX and not listed\n", ev_over));
		return TRUE;
	}

	/*
	  BUILD 56.  ent_cursor toggles the mouse mode (MOUSE3 does the same while the
	  overlay is up).  ent_click, ent_copyrow and ent_copyall are the scripted
	  forms of the three things the mouse does, so a testrun cfg drives the exact
	  code paths a hand does -- see the cursor essay.
	*/
	if (argv(0) == "ent_cursor")
	{
		if (ui_ev_cursor)
		{
			Ev_CursorOff();
			print("^5ent_cursor^7: off\n");
		}
		else
			Ev_CursorOn();
		return TRUE;
	}

	if (argv(0) == "ent_click")
	{
		if (!ui_ev_cursor)
		{
			print("^3ent_click^7: take the cursor first (ent_cursor)\n");
			return TRUE;
		}
		/*
		  No arguments means SCREEN CENTRE, sized in Ev_Draw.  A cfg cannot know
		  the virtual screen size, and at the true centre the mouse ray is the
		  crosshair ray -- so `ent_click` must select exactly what ent_here does,
		  which is the control that proves the unproject mapping.  A guessed
		  centre would make that control meaningless.
		*/
		if (argv(1) == "")
			ev_click_centre = TRUE;
		else
			ev_mouse = [stof(argv(1)), stof(argv(2))];
		ev_mouse_seen = TRUE;
		ev_click_pending = TRUE;
		return TRUE;
	}

	if (argv(0) == "ent_copyrow")
	{
		Ev_CopyRow(stof(argv(1)));
		return TRUE;
	}

	if (argv(0) == "ent_copyall")
	{
		Ev_CopyAll();
		return TRUE;
	}

	return FALSE;
};
